=== layout: subsection

# 36. Patent Trolls and Overbroad Claims

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Questions (1 of 3)

@body

- What are patent trolls, and how do they differ from traditional patent holders?
- Discuss how the business models of patent trolls impact innovation, particularly in tech and biotechnology, and consider why biotech may be especially vulnerable.
- What are some notable cases involving patent trolls or overly broad patents in technology?
- Examples include: BlackBerry v. NTP, Inc. and Eolas Technologies Inc. v. Microsoft. How did these cases highlight challenges in patent enforcement and reform?

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Questions (2 of 3)

@body

- What are some examples of "silly" or trivial patents that illustrate the sometimes excessive scope of patent claims?
- Consider how these cases underscore weaknesses in the patent approval process. Examples include: (1) Apple v. Samsung over the "Slide to Unlock" patent, (2) Amazon v. Barnes & Noble over the 1-click buy patent, and (3) Oracle v. Google over the Java API.
- What steps can be taken within the biotechnology sector to protect against patent trolling and avoid stifling innovation?

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Questions (3 of 3)

@body

- Discuss both legal strategies and industry-based solutions, such as cross-licensing agreements or shared patent pools.
- How do patent trolls increase costs and delay the development of new biotechnological products or methods?
- Consider the downstream effects on smaller biotech companies and how these effects could impact competition, innovation, and accessibility in the biotech industry.

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Enforcement as a Business Model

@body

- A patent assertion entity primarily earns money by asserting or licensing patent rights rather than making the covered product.
- The label patent troll is argumentative. Non-practicing owners also include universities, individual inventors, and other organizations with different goals.
- Evaluate the claim, ownership, notice, litigation conduct, and effect on development.
- A disputed patent can be costly even when the accused company believes the claim is weak.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Keep the Type of Case Straight

@body

- NTP's litigation against Research In Motion is a patent-enforcement example associated with BlackBerry.
- Apple/Samsung disputes included different patent issues; a phone feature's name does not identify the exact claim at issue.
- Amazon's one-click example concerns a particular claimed transaction method, not ownership of every online purchase.
- The Supreme Court's Google v. Oracle Java-API decision concerned copyright and fair use. Do not present that holding as a patent ruling.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Reducing the Cost of Weak Claims

@body

- Earlier searches and freedom-to-operate work can identify potential conflicts, though they cannot guarantee that no dispute will arise.
- Licensing, cross-licensing, patent pools, insurance, challenges, and changes to a design solve different problems and have costs of their own.
- For a small biotech company, money spent on a dispute can displace experiments, validation, or manufacturing work.
- Compare legitimate compensation for invention with strategies that impose costs without a corresponding technical contribution.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Can You Patent Playground Fun?

@body

- U.S. patent 6,368,227, issued in 2002, described a method of swinging sideways on a suspended swing.
- Read a specific claim before deciding whether something sounds novel or obvious. What earlier playground activity would you look for as prior art?
- The example makes the instructor's silly-patent question concrete. A granted patent is not a guarantee of validity, and its existence does not establish a present enforceable right.
- Now ask the harder biotechnology version: how broad should a claim to a familiar tool's new use be?

@citation

https://patents.google.com/patent/US6368227B1/en

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. What Kind of Case Was It?

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Example | The Dispute to Identify |
| --- | --- |
| NTP / Research In Motion | Patents associated with wireless email |
| Eolas / Microsoft | Patents concerning interactive browser content |
| Apple / Samsung | Specific design and utility patent claims |
| Amazon / Barnes & Noble | A claimed online-purchase method |
| Google / Oracle, Supreme Court 2021 | Copyright and fair use of Java API material |

The last holding is a copyright example; a company's lawsuit history can involve several distinct rights.

@citation

https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/18-956_d18f.pdf

