=== layout: subsection

# 37. Nondisclosure Agreement Pitfalls

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- What is a confidentiality agreement or nondisclosure agreement (NDA)?
- When would you need one?
- How does one define "Confidential Information"?
- What are some things to watch out for in an NDA?
- What is a non-compete clause?
- What is a IP assignment clause?

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- What types of durations and obligations are in an NDA?
- What is a perpetual clause or obligation?
- What is an attorney's fee provision?
- How would you change them?
- How might NDAs impact academic and industry partnerships in biotechnology research and development?
- How do NDAs and confidentiality requirements affect the open communication of scientific findings and peer review in biotechnology?

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Define Information and Allowed Use

@body

- An NDA specifies obligations concerning defined confidential information. It does not automatically establish a partnership or transfer ownership of an invention.
- Check which parties and disclosures are covered, the permitted purpose, exclusions, duration, and what happens when information must legally be disclosed.
- Public information and independently developed knowledge need different treatment from a genuinely confidential disclosure.
- A label such as mutual does not prove that both sides receive equivalent protection.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/trade-secret-policy

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Read the Rest of the Agreement

@body

- A confidentiality obligation, an IP-assignment clause, a noncompete, and a nonsolicitation restriction do different things.
- Watch for definitions so broad that ordinary knowledge becomes confidential, or terms that reach unrelated future work.
- Check attorney-fee provisions, return or destruction duties, publication review, authorized signatories, and how disputes are handled.
- A perpetual obligation is not automatically the right term for every kind of information.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/trade-secret-policy

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Protect Collaboration and Science

@body

- A company may need to protect unpublished technical details while an academic team needs to publish methods and results.
- Agree on how review, limited delays, student work, and existing obligations will be handled before sharing the information.
- The instructor's recurring caution applies: we signed an NDA does not mean we are all good.
- Ask what additional agreement governs the actual development work, ownership, responsibilities, and commercialization.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/trade-secret-policy

=== layout: subsection

# 38. Noncompetes and Biotechnology Employees

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. Questions

@body

- What are non-compete clauses?
- Who do they benefit: the employee, the employer, or both?
- Why were they made illegal in California?
- Are they legal today in Illinois?
- In Chicago?

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. What Work Would You Give Up?

@body

- A noncompete restricts specified competitive work after employment or another relationship ends.
- An NDA restricts covered disclosures; an IP assignment allocates rights in inventions. They are not interchangeable.
- Compare the employer's interest in investments or confidential information with the employee's ability to earn a living and move knowledge into new work.
- The text, scope, duration, location, and governing law matter.

@citation

https://www.ilga.gov/legislation/ilcs/fulltext?DocName=082000900K10

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. Illinois Is Not a Blanket Yes or No

@body

- As of November 12, 2026, Illinois law generally prohibits covered employee noncompetes unless annualized earnings exceed $75,000, with additional enforceability requirements and exclusions.
- The statutory earnings threshold rises to $80,000 on January 1, 2027. Higher earnings alone do not make a covenant enforceable.
- California generally prohibits employee noncompetes subject to statutory exceptions.
- Chicago is within Illinois; do not invent a separate nationwide or citywide rule without an applicable source.

@citation

https://www.ilga.gov/legislation/ilcs/fulltext?DocName=082000900K10

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. The Federal Ban Did Not Take Effect

@body

- The FTC's 2024 nationwide noncompete rule was blocked. The FTC acceded to its vacatur and moved to dismiss its appeals in September 2025.
- That development did not erase existing state laws or every possible competition-law issue.
- An introduced bill is not an enacted statute, even if its summary contains a date that has already passed.
- For a biotechnology career, identify which clause you are considering and which current law applies before making a claim about its effect.

@citation

https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/rules/noncompete-rule

